A **part : part** ratio compares one group with the other group, e.g. pens : rulers $=2:3$.

[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"ratioBlueFill","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Selected parts distinguish the first comparison group from unselected parts."}],"reference":"ratios-compare-quantities theory.facts[0]; same original counts/geometry; selected regions or explicitly named categories only"}
\definecolor{ratioBlueFill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\foreach \x in {0,0.8} {\draw[fill=ratioBlueFill] (\x,0) rectangle ++(0.8,0.8);}
\foreach \x in {1.6,2.4,3.2} {\draw[fill=white] (\x,0) rectangle ++(0.8,0.8);}
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,mirror,amplitude=6pt}] (0,-0.15) -- (1.6,-0.15) node[midway,below=10pt] {$2$};
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,mirror,amplitude=6pt}] (1.6,-0.15) -- (4,-0.15) node[midway,below=10pt] {$3$};
\node at (2,-1.6) {pens : rulers $=2:3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **part : whole** ratio compares one group with the total instead of the other group, e.g. pens : all stationery $=2:5$.

